--How many pods exist on the system?
	* kubectl get pods -A
	* kubectl get pods -n default

--Create a new pods using the nginx image
	* kubectl run nginx --image=nginx

--How many Pods are created in the default namespace?
	* kubectl get pods -n default

--Which image is specified for the pods whose names begin with the newpods prefix
	* kubectl describe pods newpod-xyz -n default
		- Look under the Contianers section to see the image that is being pulled

--Which nodes are the pods placed on?
	* kubectl describe pods newpod-xyz -n default
		- Look at the node section and it will tell you which node the pod was placed on 

--We just created a new pod named webapp. How many containers are part of the webapp pod?
	* kubectl get pod webapp -n default
	- Ready 1/2 --the so the answer is two
	- so 2 containers but only one is running 

--What images are used in the new webapp pod?
	* kubectl describe pod webapp -n default
		- Look in the container section to see what images are being pulled

--What is the state of the contaienr agentx in the pod webapp?
	* kubectl describe pod webapp -n default
		- Look at the bottom of the section to see why the contianer is not running

--What does the ready column in the output of the kubectl getpdos command indicate?
	* kubectl get pods
		- Ready containers in pod / Total containers in pod

--Delete the webapp pod...
	* kubectl delete pod webapp -n default

--Create a new pod with the name redis and the image redis123
	* kubectl run redis --image=redis123 --dry-run=client -o yaml > redis-definition.yaml
	* kubectl create -f redis-definitaion.yaml
	* kubectl get pods
